The matrix is scanned one row at a time. Along with the height of each column, the code stores the first usable column on the left and the first blocked column on the right. This gives the width available for each height without using a stack.

A zero resets a boundary because a rectangle cannot cross it. The input matrix is only read, so it is not changed.

```python
class Solution:
    def maximalAreaOfSubMatrixOfAll1(self, matrix):
        if not matrix or not matrix[0]:
            return 0

        rows = len(matrix)
        columns = len(matrix[0])
        heights = [0] * columns
        left = [0] * columns
        right = [columns] * columns
        answer = 0

        for row in range(rows):
            for column in range(columns):
                if matrix[row][column] == 1:
                    heights[column] += 1
                else:
                    heights[column] = 0

            current_left = 0
            for column in range(columns):
                if heights[column] > 0:
                    left[column] = max(left[column], current_left)
                else:
                    left[column] = 0
                    current_left = column + 1

            current_right = columns
            for column in range(columns - 1, -1, -1):
                if heights[column] > 0:
                    right[column] = min(right[column], current_right)
                else:
                    right[column] = columns
                    current_right = column

            for column in range(columns):
                width = right[column] - left[column]
                answer = max(answer, heights[column] * width)

        return answer
```

For example, with heights `[2, 2, 0, 1]`, the first two columns have a usable width of 2, so they form an area of 4. The zero stops that rectangle from reaching the fourth column.

An empty matrix or a matrix with no columns returns 0 before any indexing is done.

Time complexity: O(rows * columns).
Space complexity: O(columns).

This version keeps the calculation direct. For every possible bottom row, `height[column]` stores how many 1s are stacked above that position. Then each possible left edge is extended to the right while keeping the smallest height seen.

It is slower than the stack method, but the rectangle calculation is easy to follow. If the current minimum height is 3 and the width is 4, the rectangle area is 12.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maximalAreaOfSubMatrixOfAll1(int[][] matrix) {
        if (matrix == null || matrix.length == 0 || matrix[0].length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int rows = matrix.length;
        int columns = matrix[0].length;
        int[] height = new int[columns];
        int answer = 0;

        for (int bottom = 0; bottom < rows; bottom++) {
            for (int column = 0; column < columns; column++) {
                if (matrix[bottom][column] == 1) {
                    height[column]++;
                } else {
                    height[column] = 0;
                }
            }

            for (int left = 0; left < columns; left++) {
                int minimumHeight = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

                for (int right = left; right < columns; right++) {
                    minimumHeight = Math.min(minimumHeight, height[right]);
                    int width = right - left + 1;
                    answer = Math.max(answer, minimumHeight * width);
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

For a small dry run, heights `[1, 1, 1]` give an area of 3. If the next row changes them to `[2, 2, 0]`, the best area becomes 4.

Time complexity: O(rows * columns * columns).
Space complexity: O(columns).

Here, `width[row][column]` records the number of continuous 1s ending at that cell and going left. Every cell is treated as the bottom-right corner of a possible rectangle. Moving upward keeps the smallest width, which gives the rectangle width for each possible top row.

This avoids a separate histogram helper and is fairly simple to inspect. The width table uses extra memory, but the original matrix stays unchanged.

```go
func maximalAreaOfSubMatrixOfAll1(matrix [][]int) int {
	if len(matrix) == 0 || len(matrix[0]) == 0 {
		return 0
	}

	rows := len(matrix)
	columns := len(matrix[0])
	width := make([][]int, rows)
	for row := 0; row < rows; row++ {
		width[row] = make([]int, columns)
	}

	answer := 0
	for row := 0; row < rows; row++ {
		for column := 0; column < columns; column++ {
			if matrix[row][column] == 1 {
				width[row][column] = 1
				if column > 0 {
					width[row][column] += width[row][column-1]
				}
			}

			minimumWidth := columns
			for top := row; top >= 0 && matrix[top][column] == 1; top-- {
				if width[top][column] < minimumWidth {
					minimumWidth = width[top][column]
				}

				height := row - top + 1
				area := minimumWidth * height
				if area > answer {
					answer = area
				}
			}
		}
	}

	return answer
}
```

Time complexity: O(rows * rows * columns) in the worst case.
Space complexity: O(rows * columns).

Each row can be treated as the base of a histogram. The height of a column is the number of continuous 1s ending at that row. After building the heights, a monotonic stack finds the largest rectangle in that histogram.

The extra zero at the end makes the stack process all remaining bars. For example, after a row creates heights `[2, 2, 0]`, the stack calculates an area of 4 before moving on.

```cpp
class Solution
{
public:
    int largestHistogram(vector<int> &heights) {
        stack<int> st;
        int best = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i <= (int)heights.size(); i++) {
            int currentHeight = (i == (int)heights.size()) ? 0 : heights[i];

            while (!st.empty() && heights[st.top()] > currentHeight) {
                int height = heights[st.top()];
                st.pop();

                int leftIndex = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top();
                int width = i - leftIndex - 1;
                best = max(best, height * width);
            }

            st.push(i);
        }

        return best;
    }

    int maximalAreaOfSubMatrixOfAll1(vector<vector<int>> &matrix){
        if (matrix.empty() || matrix[0].empty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        int rows = matrix.size();
        int columns = matrix[0].size();
        vector<int> heights(columns, 0);
        int answer = 0;

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
            for (int column = 0; column < columns; column++) {
                if (matrix[row][column] == 1) {
                    heights[column]++;
                } else {
                    heights[column] = 0;
                }
            }

            answer = max(answer, largestHistogram(heights));
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: O(rows * columns).
Space complexity: O(columns).

This is a basic exhaustive check. A prefix sum table tells how many 1s are inside any rectangle in constant time. The code tries every pair of rows and every pair of columns, then keeps the rectangle only when its sum equals its area.

The method is not meant for very large matrices, but it is useful as a straightforward reference implementation. It also handles a matrix filled with zeros without any special inner-loop logic.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    public int MaximalAreaOfSubMatrixOfAll1(int[][] matrix){
        if (matrix == null || matrix.Length == 0 || matrix[0].Length == 0)
        {
            return 0;
        }

        int rows = matrix.Length;
        int columns = matrix[0].Length;
        int[,] prefix = new int[rows + 1, columns + 1];

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++)
        {
            for (int column = 0; column < columns; column++)
            {
                prefix[row + 1, column + 1] =
                    matrix[row][column]
                    + prefix[row, column + 1]
                    + prefix[row + 1, column]
                    - prefix[row, column];
            }
        }

        int answer = 0;

        for (int top = 0; top < rows; top++)
        {
            for (int bottom = top; bottom < rows; bottom++)
            {
                for (int left = 0; left < columns; left++)
                {
                    for (int right = left; right < columns; right++)
                    {
                        int sum = prefix[bottom + 1, right + 1]
                                - prefix[top, right + 1]
                                - prefix[bottom + 1, left]
                                + prefix[top, left];

                        int height = bottom - top + 1;
                        int width = right - left + 1;
                        int area = height * width;

                        if (sum == area && area > answer)
                        {
                            answer = area;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

An important edge case is a single cell. It returns 1 when that cell is 1 and 0 when it is 0. A completely empty matrix also returns 0.

Time complexity: O(rows squared * columns squared).
Space complexity: O(rows * columns).

